Scott Wood wrote:
> What is the rationale for automatically selling items that are
> dropped accidentally in shops?  When you voluntarily drop an item,
> you don't have to choose between dropping it and selling it; the
> shopkeeper is just fine with items he doesn't own being on the floor. 
[...]
> Does it date back to a time when all items on the floor in a shop
> belonged to the shopkeeper, or is it a deliberate annoyance?

      Yes (to both).  It used to be that the shopkeeper would
automatically purchase any item dropped in a shop (if it was
type of item that he was interested in selling); there wasn't
any prompting about whether to sell.  Then when selectively
selling stuff was implemented, it was a deliberate design
decision for items which are involuntarily dropped to be
handled the old way.  The program goes out of its way (the
sellobj_state stuff in shk.c and do.c) to make sure shops
behave like this.


